<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present the results of <span id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">culture-specific</span> neurons for models not shown in Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Culture-Specific Neurons ‣ 4 Experiment and Analysis ‣ Neuron-Level Analysis of Cultural Understanding in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.
First, <a href="#A5.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Appendix E Results of Culture-Specific Neurons ‣ Neuron-Level Analysis of Cultural Understanding in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 14</span></a> shows the number of neurons identified by CULNIG-specific for each country.
It is natural that the numbers are proportional to the total number of neurons (see <a href="#A2.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Appendix B Model Details ‣ Neuron-Level Analysis of Cultural Understanding in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 12</span></a>) because the initial candidate neurons are the top 0.3% neurons ranked by attribution score.
Subsequently, <span id="A5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">culture-specific</span> neurons are refined by <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CRC}_{\text{neur}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>CRC</mtext><mtext>neur</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CRC}_{\text{neur}}</annotation></semantics></math> and z-score, which may make the difference between countries.
In the table, the number of neurons corresponding to South Korea tends to be large, indicating that models possess more dedicated neurons for South Korean culture than others.</p>
</div>
